WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.275
Start with what the engine actually does.

2
00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:05.862
SQL Server doesn't check the
password for a Windows login.

3
00:00:05.862 --> 00:00:08.374
It trusts the Windows principal token,

4
00:00:08.374 --> 00:00:12.849
and Windows Authentication is always
available; it can't be disabled.

5
00:00:12.849 --> 00:00:16.287
With Integrated Security,
the driver hands off to SSPI,

6
00:00:16.287 --> 00:00:18.960
the Security Support Provider Interface,

7
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:23.925
and its Negotiate package picks Kerberos
unless one of the systems can't use it.

8
00:00:23.925 --> 00:00:28.993
The client asks the KDC on a domain
controller for a service ticket to the

9
00:00:28.993 --> 00:00:33.384
instance's SPN, which for TCP
names the SQL Server service class,

10
00:00:33.384 --> 00:00:36.425
the host's fully qualified
name and the port.

11
00:00:36.425 --> 00:00:39.737
The engine validates the ticket
with the service account's key.

12
00:00:39.737 --> 00:00:44.412
No password crosses the network, and
Kerberos authenticates both ends.

13
00:00:44.412 --> 00:00:49.150
The table is Microsoft's authentication
defaults, and it's the one to remember.

14
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.990
When the SPN maps to the correct account,

15
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:56.637
remote connections use Kerberos,
but local connections use NTLM.

16
00:00:56.637 --> 00:01:01.788
When the SPN is missing or the
lookup fails, both use NTLM.

17
00:01:01.788 --> 00:01:05.350
When it's on the wrong account,
authentication fails outright.

18
00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:09.650
That silent fallback is the most
consequential fault in the inheritance.

19
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:14.662
The engine logs a failed SPN registration
at startup, but not which protocol each

20
00:01:14.662 --> 00:01:19.525
connection used; only the auth scheme
column of the connections DMV shows that.

21
00:01:19.525 --> 00:01:22.664
And none of these paths
produces an Entra sign-in,

22
00:01:22.664 --> 00:01:25.725
so Conditional Access
has nothing to evaluate.

23
00:01:25.725 --> 00:01:28.815
The identities are anchored
in Active Directory too:

24
00:01:28.815 --> 00:01:31.484
a domain account with a
hand-rotated password,

25
00:01:31.484 --> 00:01:36.400
a group managed service account the domain
controller rotates every thirty days by

26
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.175
default, or the virtual account, which
reaches the network as the computer account.

27
00:01:41.175 --> 00:01:44.816
And the SA login, created
disabled in Windows mode,

28
00:01:44.816 --> 00:01:48.013
is often left enabled
as an emergency way in.

29
00:01:48.013 --> 00:01:49.588
None of this was a mistake.

30
00:01:49.588 --> 00:01:53.980
It was correct engineering for a
domain-joined world that no longer exists.
